<tr><td>Idling compensation unjustified</td><td>Rejected — award grounded in Ircon&rsquo;s accepted basis of settlement and the delay record</td></tr>
<tr><td>No contractual provision for interest</td><td>Rejected — silence does not displace Section 31(7)(a)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Compounding impermissible</td><td>Rejected — rate and rests within the tribunal&rsquo;s discretion where reasonable</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Takeaways for contract drafting</h2>
<p>For employers, the lesson is textual: if the commercial intent is that no interest should run on delayed payments or claims, the contract must say so in terms — a specific exclusion, not silence. For contractors, the judgment confirms that idling and prolongation claims are strengthened immeasurably by a documented parity argument: where the employer has advanced the same claim up the chain, that stance can become the measure of the claim down the chain.</p>
